From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 23:04:07 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] pdp11: a volatile bit-field is as wide as it was declared

This machine is programmed through its registers, and a register is
reached as a volatile bit-field more often than as anything else.  The
compiler was splitting those accesses into bytes:

  movb (r1),r0 ; bicb $-020,r0 ; bisb $0120,r0 ; movb r0,(r1)

Half the registers here do not take that.  Some latch on the write of
a whole word, some refuse a byte access outright, and a read-write of
one byte of a word-wide register is not what the hardware was asked
for in the first place.  It now comes out as the word it was declared
as:

  mov (r1),r0 ; bic $0360,r0 ; bis $0120,r0 ; mov r0,(r1)

Two things are needed together, which is why neither alone had any
effect.  -fstrict-volatile-bitfields tells the middle-end to keep the
access at the declared width, and is now the default here
(-fno-strict-volatile-bitfields still gets the other behaviour).  But
expmed.cc only honours that while the memory is aligned for the mode,
and without PCC_BITFIELD_TYPE_MATTERS a "volatile unsigned : 4" sits
in a byte-aligned container -- so the access narrowed to a byte
anyway, flag or no flag.

PCC_BITFIELD_TYPE_MATTERS changes structure layout, which is an ABI
question, but barely one on a machine whose int is 16 bits: a field
only moves when it would straddle a 16-bit unit, and a struct that
pads for that was already being rounded up to its alignment.  A char
followed by a one-bit field is two bytes either way; a char followed
by a twelve-bit field is four, as it was.

The cost is on the other side: a non-volatile bit-field in one byte is
now a word read-modify-write too, four bytes of code more than the
byte form.  The upstream patch this came from avoids that with an
insvhi expander that fails for exactly that case; it is not taken
here, because the rest of that expander is written for a machine
without EIS and this one has it.

The idea is from prcoder-1/digger-bk0010's GCC patch for the BK-0010,
which needs the same thing for the same reason.

Checked in gcc.target/pdp11/volatile-bitfield.c: the write of a
volatile field is mov/bic/bis/mov with no byte access anywhere in it.
The examples here build byte for byte as before.
---
 gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.cc                     | 11 +++++++
 gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.h                      | 10 ++++++
 .../gcc.target/pdp11/volatile-bitfield.c      | 33 +++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 54 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/volatile-bitfield.c

diff --git a/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.cc b/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.cc
index 41265dd..017f601 100644
--- a/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.cc
+++ b/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.cc
@@ -2472,6 +2472,17 @@ pdp11_option_override (void)
 	       "are alternative PDP-11 floating point options, not a pair");
       target_flags &= ~MASK_FIS;
     }
+
+  /* This machine is programmed through its registers, and a register
+     is reached as a volatile bit-field more often than as anything
+     else.  Some of them latch on the write of a whole word, some
+     refuse a byte access outright, and none of them want the two byte
+     stores the middle-end is otherwise free to split a word write
+     into.  So keep a volatile bit-field access at the width it was
+     declared with, which is what -fstrict-volatile-bitfields asks for;
+     -fno-strict-volatile-bitfields still gets the other behaviour.  */
+  if (flag_strict_volatile_bitfields < 0)
+    flag_strict_volatile_bitfields = 1;
 }
 
 static void
diff --git a/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.h b/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.h
index 73141d2..1cec135 100644
--- a/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.h
+++ b/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.h
@@ -142,6 +142,16 @@ extern const struct real_format pdp11_d_format;
    when given unaligned data.  */
 #define STRICT_ALIGNMENT 1
 
+/* A named bit-field aligns its structure to the field's declared type,
+   not to whatever container the bits happen to fit in.  This is the PCC
+   convention, and here it is what makes a volatile bit-field reach the
+   hardware as the width it was declared with: expmed.cc keeps a strict
+   volatile access at the declared mode only while the memory is aligned
+   for that mode, and without this a "volatile unsigned : 4" sits in a
+   byte-aligned container, so the access narrows to a byte.  Half this
+   machine's registers do not take a byte write.  */
+#define PCC_BITFIELD_TYPE_MATTERS true
+
 /* "HW_DIVIDE" actually means 64 by 32 bit divide.  While some PDP11
    models have hardware divide, it is for 32 by 16 bits only, so we
    call this platform "no hardware divide".  */
diff --git a/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/volatile-bitfield.c b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/volatile-bitfield.c
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..7242c77
--- /dev/null
+++ b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/volatile-bitfield.c
@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
+/* { dg-do compile { target pdp11-*-* } } */
+/* { dg-options "-O2" } */
+
+/* A device register is reached as a volatile bit-field more often than
+   as anything else, and half of this machine's registers do not take a
+   byte write: some latch on the write of a whole word, some refuse a
+   byte access outright.  So an access to a volatile bit-field has to
+   stay the width it was declared with -- a word here -- rather than be
+   narrowed to the byte the bits happen to sit in.
+
+   Two things together make that hold: PCC_BITFIELD_TYPE_MATTERS, so
+   the field's container is aligned as the declared type, and
+   -fstrict-volatile-bitfields on by default, so the middle-end keeps
+   the access at that width.  */
+
+struct reg
+{
+  volatile unsigned low : 4, high : 4, rest : 8;
+};
+
+void
+set_field (struct reg *r)
+{
+  r->high = 5;
+}
+
+/* Read-modify-write of the whole word, and not a byte in sight.  */
+/* { dg-final { scan-assembler "mov\t" } } */
+/* { dg-final { scan-assembler "bic\t" } } */
+/* { dg-final { scan-assembler "bis\t" } } */
+/* { dg-final { scan-assembler-not "movb\t" } } */
+/* { dg-final { scan-assembler-not "bicb\t" } } */
+/* { dg-final { scan-assembler-not "bisb\t" } } */
